From: Alex Chong <achong@meta.com>
Date: Fri, 1 May 2026 10:01:56 -1000
Subject: [PATCH 60/63] [cuttlefish] Fix host process cleanup when QEMU exits
 unexpectedly
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Summary:
When a user closes the QEMU display window (Cmd+Q or red X on macOS),
QEMU exits but all associated host processes remain running indefinitely.
This leaves the Cuttlefish instance in a broken state: the VM is gone
but dozens of orphaned processes persist, and run_cvd stays alive. In
multi-instance scenarios, this also leaks resources and can cause port
conflicts on subsequent launches.

## Root cause

Two bugs prevent cleanup when the VMM (QEMU) exits:

### Bug 1: Monitor child deadlocks

ProcessMonitor forks a monitor child that runs two concurrent tasks:

  - MonitorLoop: blocks in wait(), detects child process exits
  - ReadMonitorSocketLoop: blocks in ReceiveMessage(), waits for
    parent commands (kStop, kSuspend, kResume)

When QEMU exits, MonitorLoop detects it (is_critical=true), sets
running=false, and returns. The code then calls parent_comms.get()
to wait for ReadMonitorSocketLoop — but that thread is permanently
blocked in a socket read that will never return. Deadlock.
StopSubprocesses() is never called. All host processes stay alive.

### Bug 2: Parent run_cvd never exits

Even if Bug 1 were fixed, the parent run_cvd process stays alive
because it is blocked in SharedFD::Accept() on the launcher monitor
socket. Nothing notifies it that the monitor child exited.

## Before (broken)

```
  QEMU exits (Cmd+Q / red X)
       |
       v
  MonitorLoop: wait() returns, detects critical exit
       |
       v
  MonitorLoop: sets running=false, returns
       |
       v
  parent_comms.get()  <-- DEADLOCK
       |                   ReadMonitorSocketLoop is blocked
       |                   in ReceiveMessage() forever.
       x                   Nothing wakes it up.

  StopSubprocesses() -- NEVER REACHED

  Meanwhile in the parent run_cvd process:

  ServerLoop: Accept()  <-- BLOCKED FOREVER
       |                    No mechanism to detect
       x                    monitor child death.

  Result: all host processes orphaned, run_cvd alive but useless.
```

## After (fixed)

```
  QEMU exits (Cmd+Q / red X)
       |
       v
  MonitorLoop: wait() returns, detects critical exit
       |
       v
  MonitorLoop: sets running=false, returns
       |
       v
  child_sock_->Shutdown(SHUT_RDWR)
       |    Interrupts ReadMonitorSocketLoop's blocking
       |    ReceiveMessage() -- the read returns EOF/error.
       v
  parent_comms.get()  <-- returns (with expected error)
       |
       v
  StopSubprocesses()  <-- kills all host processes in reverse order
       |
       v
  Monitor child exits (std::exit(0))
       |    Socket pair child end closed by kernel.
       |    Parent's end gets EOF.
       v
  ServerLoop: Select() detects monitor_fd readable (EOF)
       |
       v
  WaitForMonitorExit() -- waitpid() on monitor child
       |
       v
  Parent run_cvd exits (std::exit(0))

  Result: all host processes cleaned up, run_cvd exits,
          other instances unaffected.
```

## Architecture (Select-based ServerLoop)

```
  Parent run_cvd (ServerLoop)
  ============================
       |
       |  Select() watches two fds:
       |
       |--- server_ (launcher_monitor_socket)
       |       Accepts connections from cvd stop,
       |       cvd restart, cvd powerwash, etc.
       |
       |--- monitor_fd (parent end of socket pair)
       |       Becomes readable (EOF) when monitor
       |       child exits due to critical process
       |       death. Triggers WaitForMonitorExit()
       |       and std::exit(0).
       |
       v
  Monitor child (forked by ProcessMonitor)
  =========================================
       |
       |  Two concurrent tasks:
       |
       |--- MonitorLoop (main thread)
       |       Blocks in wait(). When critical process
       |       (QEMU) exits: sets running=false, breaks.
       |
       |--- ReadMonitorSocketLoop (async thread)
       |       Blocks in ReceiveMessage(). Handles kStop,
       |       kSuspend, kResume from parent.
       |
       |  After MonitorLoop returns:
       |    1. Shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) on child socket
       |       to unblock ReadMonitorSocketLoop
       |    2. Join the async thread
       |    3. StopSubprocesses() in reverse order
       |    4. Exit
       |
       v
  Managed subprocesses (per-instance)
  ====================================
       QEMU, webRTC, logcat_receiver, kernel_log_monitor,
       secure_env, modem_simulator, casimir, netsimd,
       sensors_simulator, console_forwarder, ...
       (~35 processes per instance)
```

## Changes

### process_monitor.h
- Add child_sock_ member: raw SharedFD of child socket, used to
  call Shutdown() to interrupt ReadMonitorSocketLoop
- Add parent_monitor_sock_ member: raw SharedFD of parent socket,
  exposed to ServerLoop for Select()-based exit detection
- Add GetMonitorFd(): returns parent_monitor_sock_ for Select()
- Add WaitForMonitorExit(): waitpid() without sending kStop,
  for when the monitor child has already exited on its own

### process_monitor.cc
- StartAndMonitorProcesses(): store child_sock_ and
  parent_monitor_sock_ before wrapping in SharedFdChannel.
  Close parent_sock in child process to avoid fd leak.
- MonitorRoutine(): after MonitorLoop returns, call
  child_sock_->Shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) to interrupt the blocking
  ReceiveMessage(). Handle parent_comms error gracefully since
  the shutdown intentionally causes it. StopSubprocesses() now
  always executes.
- Implement GetMonitorFd() and WaitForMonitorExit().

### server_loop_impl.cpp
- Add shared_select.h include.
- Replace bare Accept() loop with Select()-based loop watching
  both server_ and monitor_fd. When monitor_fd is readable (EOF
  from monitor child exit), call WaitForMonitorExit() and exit.
  This also addresses the existing TODO on the old Accept() call.

## Correctness

- kStop path (cvd stop): ReadMonitorSocketLoop receives kStop,
  sets running=false, wakes MonitorLoop. MonitorLoop exits.
  Shutdown() is harmless (thread already exited). StopSubprocesses
  runs. Monitor child exits. Parent's StopMonitoredProcesses()
  was already handling this synchronously. No behavior change.

- Critical exit path (QEMU killed): MonitorLoop exits. Shutdown()
  unblocks ReadMonitorSocketLoop. StopSubprocesses runs. Monitor
  child exits. Parent detects EOF via Select(), exits. Fixed.

- Multi-instance: each run_cvd has its own ProcessMonitor and
  forked monitor child. Killing one QEMU only affects that
  instance's process tree. Other instances are unaffected.

- Platform: Shutdown(), Select(), and socket-pair EOF detection
  all work identically on Linux and macOS. No platform-specific
  code added.

Test Plan:
Test: launched two Cuttlefish instances with QEMU on macOS,
killed cvd-1 via Cmd+Q, verified cvd-1 processes cleaned up
and cvd-2 remained fully operational. Then killed cvd-2,
verified complete cleanup with zero orphaned processes.
